      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $E$ denote the given expression. Use $\\sin x\\cos x=\\frac{1}{2}\\sin(2x)$ first at $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{16}$ and then at $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{8}$. Each application introduces a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE&=4\\,\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{8}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{8}\\right)\\\\&=2\\,\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe terminal sine is $\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE&=2\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\&=\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
